  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>A physical data-centre audit covers power, cooling, access control, fire suppression and environmental monitoring</li><li>DCIM platforms track the live estate continuously; they are not built for a one-off facility walk-through</li><li>The value of the tool is clean on-floor evidence capture: photo, reading, timestamp and location</li><li>Quickler suits the physical walk-through and evidence trail, not the cyber or logical audit</li><li>The audit's validity comes from the competent auditor and the standard, not the tool</li></ul></div><h2>What data centre audit software is for</h2><p>A physical data-centre or comms-facility audit checks that the building keeps the equipment running and secure. That covers power resilience (UPS, generators, dual feeds), cooling (CRAC and CRAH units, hot and cold aisle containment), physical access control, fire detection and suppression, and environmental monitoring for temperature and humidity. Uptime Institute tier concepts describe how much redundancy a facility is designed to carry; an audit records the state of what is actually installed against that intent.</p><p>Software does not make the audit sound. The competent auditor and the standard do. What software does is make the physical walk-through easy to record: a photo of the fire panel, a UPS load reading, a note against a failed door contact, each stamped with time and location. A report typed up from memory once you have left the floor is always the weaker report.</p><h2>The three types of data centre audit tool</h2><h3>1. DCIM and monitoring platforms</h3><p>Sunbird and Nlyte are Data Centre Infrastructure Management platforms. They track the live estate continuously: power draw per rack, cooling capacity, asset location, port mapping. If you own and run a facility, they are the backbone of operations. They are not built for a one-off physical audit. If you already run DCIM, feed its data into the audit rather than expecting it to be the audit tool.</p><h3>2. General inspection apps</h3><p>iAuditor (SafetyCulture) and GoAudits are checklist apps for any inspection. A facility template is one of thousands. They work and they are flexible, but the app must be installed on each auditor's phone, they need training, and the licence is per seat. For a firm that audits a handful of sites a quarter, that is a lot of standing cost for occasional use.</p><h3>3. Conversation-based workflows</h3><p>The newer category drops the app. The auditor receives the facility walk-through as a WhatsApp conversation. Each prompt arrives as a message; they reply with text, a voice note or a photo. The record generates automatically and the office sees it on a dashboard. The advantage is the absence of friction: the auditor already uses WhatsApp, so there is nothing to install and nothing to learn. Quickler runs in this category, for the physical walk-through and the evidence trail.</p><h2>Comparison: the main options</h2><ul><li><strong>Sunbird / Nlyte</strong> - DCIM platforms. Continuous estate telemetry. Powerful for running a facility, heavy for a one-off audit.</li><li><strong>iAuditor</strong> - General inspection. From ~£19/seat/month. App install and template setup required.</li><li><strong>GoAudits</strong> - General inspection. From ~£10/seat/month. App install required.</li><li><strong>Quickler</strong> - WhatsApp workflow. £20 per active user per month, dormant users free. Only pay for who works. Free tier of 20 reports a month. Runs in WhatsApp, no install. Physical audit only.</li><li><strong>Paper and spreadsheet</strong> - Cheap, familiar, hard to search, no photo trail.</li></ul><p>Pricing is approximate and changes. Check each vendor's current rates directly.</p><h2>What a physical facility audit covers</h2><p>Walk the facility and record the installed state: UPS and generator readiness, fuel levels and last test dates; cooling units and aisle containment; physical access control, door contacts and visitor logs; fire detection and suppression, including panel state and last service; and environmental monitoring for temperature and humidity against thresholds. Capture each observation where you see it, with a photo and a timestamp, not reconstructed later.</p><h2>Where Quickler helps and where it does not</h2><p>Quickler is good at the on-site physical audit: capturing what you observe as you walk a facility, with photo evidence, a timestamp and a clean shareable record. It suits power, cooling, access, fire and environmental checks, and the physical controls behind a standard like ISO 27001. It does not run the logical or cyber side: vulnerability scans, firewall review, log analysis or penetration testing. Those belong to dedicated security tooling and a qualified assessor. If your audit is mostly logical, Quickler is the wrong tool.</p><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> the audit's validity comes from the competent auditor and the standard, not from any tool. Quickler makes the physical evidence easy to gather; it does not certify anything. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if you are unsure whether your audit fits.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is the best software for a physical data centre audit in the UK?</h3><p>For the physical walk-through, the options are DCIM platforms (Sunbird, Nlyte) if you already run the estate, general inspection apps (iAuditor, GoAudits) for checklist audits, or a WhatsApp workflow like Quickler for capturing evidence on the floor without an app install. DCIM suits live estate management; a conversation workflow suits an occasional facility audit across several sites.</p><h3>Can Quickler run a full ISO 27001 audit?</h3><p>No. Quickler suits the physical and environmental security controls in ISO 27001 Annex A, the on-site walk-through part. It does not cover the logical, cyber or management-system clauses, which need a qualified assessor and dedicated tooling. Use Quickler for the physical evidence, not the certification.</p><h3>What does a data centre facility audit actually cover?</h3><p>A physical facility audit covers power resilience (UPS, generators, feeds), cooling (CRAC and CRAH units, aisle containment), physical access control and visitor logs, fire detection and suppression, and environmental monitoring for temperature and humidity. The point is to record the installed state against the design intent, with evidence.</p><h3>Do auditors need to install an app to use Quickler?</h3><p>No. Quickler runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The auditor receives each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, voice notes or photos, and the record generates automatically. Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/comms-room-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Comms room inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/iso-27001-physical-security-audit-uk.html\">ISO 27001 physical security audit</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/dr-site-audit-checklist-uk.html\">DR site audit checklist</a></li></ul>",
